# Match other developers on staging first

Several people push to `develop`. That branch is the staging line. Before the first edit in a session, match it. Do this once per session, not again on later messages unless the user asks.

## Check

```bash
git fetch origin
git status -sb -- . ':(exclude)node_modules'
git log --format='%h %an %s' HEAD..origin/develop
```

Tell the user who pushed what you did not have yet. If the log is empty, say `develop` already matches `origin/develop`.

## Update the checkout

- Stay on `develop`. Fast-forward with `git pull --ff-only origin develop`.
- Local work that is only line endings may be restored when it blocks that pull. Real uncommitted edits stay. Do not stash, reset, or delete them.
- If `develop` has diverged from `origin/develop`, stop. Do not `reset --hard`, rebase, or force-push.
- Ignore dirt in `node_modules`, `.env*`, `supabase/.temp`, and the stray `node` log. Do not commit those.

## Staging database

- New files under `supabase/migrations/` that staging `scvojtvgnkmbupvyslmb` does not already have: name them. Apply them on staging only when the set is small and this task needs the database.
- Staging may already list versions that are not files in git. Leave those. Do not repair or revert them.
- Do not run `npx supabase db push`. Do not touch production `levwrmvqdntngeasrtnb` or `main`.

- If those commits change `package.json` or `package-lock.json`, run `npm install` at the repo root before starting the app.
- If a dev server was already running, stop it and start `npm run dev` again. Vite must be running with its working directory `apps/admin`. A server left on the repo root serves `/` as 404 because `index.html` now lives in `apps/admin`.

Then do the user's task on that updated `develop`.
